---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `love.graphics.setColor(1, 0.7, 0.1)`;

const Figure2 = `love.graphics.rectangle("fill", 100, 100, 200, 120)
love.graphics.rectangle("line", 100, 100, 200, 120)
love.graphics.circle("fill", 200, 160, 30)
love.graphics.line(0, 0, 800, 600)`;

const Figure3 = `love.graphics.setColor(1, 0, 0)
love.graphics.rectangle("fill", 10, 10, 50, 50)
love.graphics.rectangle("fill", 70, 10, 50, 50)`;

const Task1 = `-- main.lua

function love.load()
  love.graphics.setBackgroundColor(0.05, 0.05, 0.1)
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.setColor(0.2, 0.6, 0.9)
  love.graphics.rectangle("fill", 100, 100, 200, 120)
end`;

const Task2 = `-- main.lua

function love.load()
  love.graphics.setBackgroundColor(0.05, 0.05, 0.1)
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.setColor(0.2, 0.6, 0.9)
  love.graphics.rectangle("fill", 100, 100, 200, 120)

  love.graphics.setColor(1, 1, 1)
  love.graphics.setLineWidth(3)
  love.graphics.rectangle("line", 100, 100, 200, 120)

  love.graphics.setColor(1, 0.7, 0.1)
  love.graphics.circle("fill", 200, 160, 30)
end`;

const Task3 = `-- main.lua

function love.load()
  love.graphics.setBackgroundColor(0.05, 0.05, 0.1)
end

function love.draw()
  love.graphics.setColor(0.2, 0.6, 0.9)
  love.graphics.rectangle("fill", 100, 100, 200, 120)

  love.graphics.setColor(1, 1, 1)
  love.graphics.setLineWidth(3)
  love.graphics.rectangle("line", 100, 100, 200, 120)

  love.graphics.setColor(1, 0.7, 0.1)
  love.graphics.circle("fill", 200, 160, 30)

  love.graphics.setColor(1, 1, 1)
  love.graphics.print("REACTOR NOMINAL", love.graphics.getWidth() / 2 - 55, 260)
end`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a
          href="/courses/lua-programming/"
          class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3"
        >
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.2: Drawing to the Screen</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Concept -->
        <section id="concept">
          <h2>The Idea</h2>

          <p>
            Two facts about the canvas, and then some shapes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The origin is the top-left, and y grows
            downward.</b> A larger y is <i>lower</i> on the screen. This trips
            everyone once and then never again.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Colors run from 0 to 1, not 0 to 255.</b>{" "}
            This is the single most common thing to get wrong, because most of
            the internet's LÖVE code predates the change and uses 0&ndash;255.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure1}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            Red, green, blue, and optionally alpha, each from 0 to 1. If you find
            a snippet online that says{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="setColor(255, 180, 25)"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, divide everything by 255. <b>Passing 255 does not error</b>{" "}
            &mdash; it clamps to 1, so every color comes out pure white and
            nothing tells you why.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Shapes</h3>

          <Code
            code={Figure2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <ul>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet
                code="rectangle(mode, x, y, width, height)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              &mdash; x and y are the <b>top-left corner</b>, not the middle.
            </li>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet
                code="circle(mode, x, y, radius)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              &mdash; here x and y <i>are</i> the middle. The inconsistency is
              real and worth remembering.
            </li>
            <li>
              <CodeSnippet
                code="line(x1, y1, x2, y2, ...)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              &mdash; as many points as you like, joined in order.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The first argument is always{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`"fill"`}
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            or{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code={`"line"`}
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            &mdash; solid or outline. Draw the fill first and the outline second,
            or the fill covers the outline.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Drawing is a state machine</h3>

          <p>
            This is the part that behaves unlike anything in Unit 1.{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="setColor"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            does not draw anything and does not belong to any shape.{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">It changes a setting that stays changed</b>{" "}
            until something changes it back.
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Figure3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mb-3"
          />

          <p>
            <b>Both squares are red.</b> One{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="setColor"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, two shapes. The same is true of{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="setLineWidth"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="setFont"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">The bug this causes is always the same
            shape:</b> something you drew is mysteriously the wrong color,
            because code <i>above</i> it set a color and never set it back. When
            that happens, read upward, not at the shape itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            The habit that prevents it is to set the color immediately before
            every group of shapes, even when you think you already know what it
            is. It costs one line and saves a hunt.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Background, and why the screen is not smeared</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="setBackgroundColor"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            belongs in{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.load"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />, not in draw &mdash; it is a setting, not a drawing call. LÖVE
            wipes the window to that color before every single{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.draw"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>Which means each frame is drawn from scratch.</b> Nothing you drew
            last frame is still there. That sounds wasteful and is the reason
            animation works at all: to move something, you do not move it &mdash;
            you draw it somewhere else next frame. Activity 2.3 is entirely built
            on that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="mt-5">Finding the middle</h3>

          <p>
            <CodeSnippet
              code="love.graphics.getWidth()"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            and{" "}
            <CodeSnippet
              code="getHeight()"
              lang={"lua"}
              theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
              inline
            />{" "}
            report the window size. <b>Use them instead of typing 800 and
            600</b>, so that changing conf.lua does not silently break your
            layout.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Keep working in the same <b class="text-sn-theme">skynest</b> folder.
            You are drawing an instrument panel.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: A filled rectangle</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Clear out Activity 2.1's text and counter.</li>
            <li>Set a blue color and fill a rectangle at 100, 100.</li>
            <li>
              Keep the dark background in{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.load"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="lua" title="main.lua" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Outline it, then add a reactor</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Draw a white <b>outline</b> at exactly the same position and size.
              Set the line width to 3 first.
            </li>
            <li>
              Add an orange filled circle in the middle of the panel. Work out
              its center from the rectangle's numbers rather than guessing.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Set the color before each shape.</b> Then, once it works, delete
              one of the{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="setColor"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              lines and see which shape changes. That is the state machine.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task2}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "10-16" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: A label, centered by arithmetic</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Set the color back to white and print a label below the panel.
            </li>
            <li>
              Position it using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.graphics.getWidth() / 2"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              rather than a hard-coded 400.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>Then change the window width in conf.lua</b> and run again. The
              label follows; the panel does not. That is the difference the
              function makes.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <Code
            code={Task3}
            frame="code"
            lang="lua"
            title="main.lua"
            ins={{ range: "17-19" }}
          />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Challenge (Optional)</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Subtracting 55 to center the text is a guess. Replace it with a
              real measurement using{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.graphics.getFont():getWidth(text)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />, and it will be exact for any string.
            </li>
            <li>
              Draw the panel from a <b>table</b> &mdash;{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="local panel = { x = 100, y = 100, w = 200, h = 120 }"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              &mdash; and use its fields everywhere. Activity 1.10, and it is how
              every object in the rest of this unit is stored.
            </li>
            <li>
              Write a{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="drawPanel(x, y, w, h)"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              function and call it three times at different positions. Your{" "}
              <CodeSnippet
                code="love.draw"
                lang={"lua"}
                theme={"material-theme-ocean"}
                inline
              />{" "}
              becomes three lines.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Check Your Work</h2>

          <p>On a near-black background you should have:</p>

          <ul>
            <li>A blue filled rectangle with a crisp white outline.</li>
            <li>An orange circle centered inside it.</li>
            <li>A white label centered under the panel.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">If everything is white, you used
            0&ndash;255 values.</b> If the outline is missing, it was drawn
            before the fill and got covered. If the circle is off-center, check
            that you added <i>half</i> the width and height to the rectangle's
            corner.
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-5">
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-light"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-1"
          >
            <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Previous
          </a>
          <a
            class="btn btn-outline-primary"
            href="/courses/lua-programming/unit-2/2-3"
          >
            Next: Movement and Delta Time <i class="si-arrow-right"></i>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
